{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-359107",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $12$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,6,\\,12.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $18$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,6,\\,9,\\,18.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $30$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,5,\\,6,\\,10,\\,15,\\,30.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $12,$ $18,$ and $30$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,6.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $6.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. The expressions $12z,$ $18z,$ and $30z$ all contain $z.$\n\nThus, the $\\text{GCF}$ of $12z,$ $18z,$ and $30z$ is $6z.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
